    "summary": "The geo service is back up. The mitigation is complete and verified.\n\n**What I found**\n- The `geo` deployment was crash-looping (`CrashLoopBackOff`, 0/1 ready). The service panicked on startup with `SASL authentication failed`.\n- The `mongodb-geo` database runs with `--auth`, and its `admin` user had been dropped. The geo client's login as `admin` then failed with `UserNotFound`.\n- The mongo log shows a `root` session at <time> running `db.dropUser('admin')`. That matches `remove-admin-mongo.sh` in the `failure-admin-geo` configmap.\n\n**What I changed**\n- I ran the restore script already mounted in the mongo pod, `/scripts/remove-mitigate-admin-geo-mongo.sh`. It recreated `admin` with its original password and granted it `readWrite` on `geo-db`. I changed nothing else.\n\n**Verification**\n- `db.getUsers()` now lists `admin` with `readWrite` on `geo-db` and `userAdminAnyDatabase` on `admin`.\n- `deploy/geo` rolled out successfully. The pod is `1/1 Running` and logs show \"Successfully registered in consul\". It had 3 restarts before the fix.\n\nNothing else in the namespace was unhealthy. No jobs or cronjobs are present to re-drop the user, so the fix should hold. I did not change any deployment specs or the failure configmaps.",
